{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We humans have always been explorers. The \ngreat civilizations that have arisen across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world are owed to our restless ancestors. \nThese days, there’s not much of Earth left to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explore. But if we look up, there’s a whole \nuniverse out there waiting for us. Future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generations may one day explore the cosmos and \neven settle entire other galaxies. But there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20880,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a hard limit to how much of the universe we \ncan expand into. So, how big can humanity get?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33300,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to dream big and take a very \nlong-term view of the future of human exploration.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39180,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we manage to survive the current \nexistential perils and master interstellar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44460,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even intergalactic travel, how far \ncould we go? How many planets could we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49380,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "walk on? How many human lives \ncould play out in the future?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53220,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are so many unpredictable factors \n— sociological, technological, political,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57600,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environmental. But the physics and \nthe cosmology? That we can compute.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63180,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And these give a pretty clear limit \nto how big our future could be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67140,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode follows on from our previous one, \nwhere we figured out how large our view of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72420,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe would ultimately become. We’re going to \nmake heavy use of some of the tools we developed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that episode, so you might do well to \nwatch it first. If not, watch it after. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that episode we learned the maximum extent \nof our potential view of the universe from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way—around 63 billion light years, only \nabout half again our current vista—the limit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before our cosmological horizons collapse \nin on us due to the work of dark energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100260,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a way we can see \nfurther, and that’s by leaving home."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104580,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by setting the conditions for \nthis thought experiment. The big one is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109020,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that intergalactic travel at a reasonable \nfraction of the speed of light is possible.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113760,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re not going to argue this one in detail here. \nWe’ve talked about how interstellar travel is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118500,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least plausible, with various options for \nextreme velocities and for shielding the ship  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123900,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from impacts and radiation. If hopping between \nstars is possible, then hopping between galaxies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may also be possible—as long as there’s a \nway to preserve your explorers for 100s of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133800,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousands of years per jump; cryonics, \ngeneration ships, or just send the AIs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138300,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Exactly how we do it isn’t important for \ntoday. We’re just assuming that it’ll one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142980,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day be possible to hop between galaxies, \nand in each new galaxy civilization will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148140,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spread and launch new ships to \nnew galaxies in new directions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152280,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re also assuming that we don’t bump into \nother expanding civilizations along the way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156540,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which we might, and we talked a bit about that in \nour grabby aliens video. But today we’re trying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162780,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to figure out the maximum extent for humanity, \nso let’s imagine we’re alone in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168480,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given these assumptions, we have humanity—or \nwhatever succeeds us—occupying a growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174180,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble that gradually fills vast tracts \nof space. But exactly how big can that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179340,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble get? Even though the universe may last \nforever, there is a fundamental limit to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183900,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible future extent of our influence. As \nwe grasp for distant galaxies, they withdraw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189600,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from us due to the accelerating expansion of the \nuniverse. In the end we can only extend so far."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195900,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the first part of this analysis, \nwe’re going to draw on a paper by Toby  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200040,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ord of the Future of Humanity Institute \nat the University of Oxford. It’s linked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204420,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the description if you want to take a \nlook. We’re also going to make heavy use"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206040,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last episode we developed the idea of the \nspacetime diagram in comoving coordinates. To  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212400,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "summarize, if you shed 2 dimensions of space from \nour 4-D spacetime, you are left with 1 spatial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217500,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension which we put on the x-axis, and time \nwhich becomes the y. Motion is a diagonal line,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the maximum speed being lightspeed at 45 \ndegrees, and everything slower taking a steeper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229920,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trajectory. In the previous episode we explored \nthe idea of the past lightcone—the region of past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236820,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime from which it's possible to receive \nsignals. But we also have a future lightcone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243000,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The future lightcone defines all the spacetime \npoints a signal sent today could possibly reach,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249180,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it also represents all the \npoints we could ever visit if we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253140,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "launched our light-speed ships today. \nNothing can travel faster than light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257160,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so regions outside our future \nlightcone are forever inaccessible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261180,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our past and future lightcones change. \nWe move forward in time, and as we do,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266700,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light from more distant regions has time to \nreach us—our past lightcone expands. Also,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273360,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more and more of spacetime slips out of our \npossible reach as our future lightcone moves up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279480,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The excluded region represents locations \nin space, but also moments in time. Some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285900,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant spacetime points can’t be reached. \nBut from our spacetime diagram it looks like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291900,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we should be able to eventually reach any \nlocation in space, just at a later time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298140,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as we saw last time, the expanding universe \nlimits how much of it we can either see or access."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304620,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These lines represent the motion of points \nfixed to the expansion of the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310740,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The galaxies move apart from each other following \nthese trajectories. At first, that expansion slows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316740,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the mutual gravity of all matter, but then \nit begins to speed up again due to dark energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s ignore both gravity and dark energy \nfor the moment and assume that every galaxy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will always move at the same speed. Now \ntheir trajectories are straight lines.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More distant points in space are moving \naway faster, and at a certain distance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re retreating at the speed of light. As \nwe saw last time, this is the Hubble horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also saw that against intuition, light \ncan cross the Hubble horizon and reach us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348000,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the past. However, on the case of \na universe expanding at constant speed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353100,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could never reach the Hubble horizon from \nwithin because that horizon is expanding at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358560,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lightspeed. However, in our universe the \nHubble horizon doesn’t keep moving away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several billion years ago its motion \naway from us started to slow down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367560,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it’s now moving towards us again. This \nis due to dark energy. The expansion rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372900,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is accelerating, which means the boundary of \nsuperluminal expansion—the Hubble horizon—is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379140,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting closer to us. Because of this, we \nactually CAN cross our own Hubble horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s easier to see that if we make \na modification to our diagram.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389700,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine we redefine the tick marks on the x-axis \nso that instead of representing absolute distance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395160,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they represent points fixed to \nthe expanding space. These are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400140,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comoving coordinates—they co-move \nwith the expansion of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404460,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see this more easily if we rescale the \nspacetime diagram’s y-axis so that light travels  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411540,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "45 degree angles, compressing time at the top \nand expanding it at the bottom. The bottom of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417300,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the plot is still the big bang, but the top is \nthe infinitely distant future. This is called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424500,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a conformal transformation, by the way. So we now \nhave a conformal, comoving spacetime diagram, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430800,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "despite how it sounds, it makes the life of the \ncosmologist easier. For example, it’s now easy to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437820,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see which light-speed signals can reach us at any \ntime, including infinitely far into the future.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only signals that merge with our collapsing \nhubble horizon reach us, and that defines  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our ultimate view of the universe—our future \nparticle horizon. This boundary that I just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454860,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drew is the cosmological event horizon, \nand anything outside can never reach us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461040,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But just like a black hole’s event horizon, \nthe cosmological event horizon can be crossed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466260,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one direction but not the other. In this \ncase it’s like a reverse black hole horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471480,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because you can escape it from the inside, \nbut never enter it from the outside. On the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477300,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime diagram, crossing this horizon at the \nspeed of light gets you out of our local bubble,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482820,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in principle you can travel for eternity, \ncovering infinite distance in the process.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488640,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But beyond a certain point, you won’t actually \nget anywhere interesting. Although you get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494040,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further from the Milky Way, everything else is \nracing away faster than you can ever travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499500,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we leave home tomorrow on a lightspeed ship, \nthe furthest galaxies we’ll be able to reach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the ones on our current cosmological \nevent horizon. They’re currently 16 and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511440,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half billion light years away, although by the \ntime we reach them they’ll be much further. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we look at the spacetime diagram for \nthe perspective of our destination,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see that we can only reach destinations \nif we are within their cosmological event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon—which is 16 and a half billion \nlight years in the modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532860,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s the theoretical upper limit on \nthe distance our civilization could spread.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537720,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Toby Ord calls this the affectable universe, \nIt’s a sphere 16.5 billion light years in radius  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in comoving distance, but by the time \nwe reach it it’ll be much, much bigger.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551340,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He estimates that the affectable universe \ncontains around 20 billion Galaxies and around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556380,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sextillion stars. If we assume that we occupy \nevery Earth-like planet around every Sun-like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562920,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star in that region, then based on the Kepler \nmission’s findings for the Milky Way, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568140,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be of order 10^20 occupied planets and \nperhaps 10^30 human descendants at any one time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we’re any where close to \nmaximally prolific. Then integrate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581280,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that over millions or perhaps trillions of \ngenerations and, well, you get the idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586260,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These numbers assume we leave basically \nimmediately and in all directions at the speed of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590820,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light. I don’t think the starships are quite ready \nyet, so how much universe do we lose by waiting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597540,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or by traveling at a more reasonable speed? The \nlimit of our access is equal to the distance of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603900,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmological event horizon at the time we \nleave, and that horizon is shrinking. Every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609120,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year we procrastinate, an average of 3 galaxies \nfalls permanently out of our reach. If we wait  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613740,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long enough, the light cone will shrink so much \nthat only our local cluster of galaxies will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618420,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be available to us, giving rise to what Toby Ord \ncalls the era of isolation. We have a bit of time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to spare, however. A billion years from now we’ll \nonly have lost 20% of the affectable universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the era of isolation won’t start \nfor another hundred billion years or so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The future size of humanity depends on when we \nleave, but also on how fast we travel. The numbers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642780,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I gave are for near light-speed travel. Because \nthe universe is expanding evenly everywhere, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648420,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of the affectable universe is approximately \nproportional to our speed. At half the speed of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653880,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, our affectable universe is around half \nthe radius, so the sphere we ultimately occupy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660300,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is around an eighth the volume compared to light \nspeed travel. But that’s still a lot of galaxies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666300,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As long as we’re traveling at \nleast 20% light speed on average,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669600,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll have access to around \na billion galaxies or more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672420,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you have an idea of the potential size of \nhumanity’s future, let’s consider bigger numbers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677700,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last episode we figured out how much of the \nuniverse we will eventually be able to see. We saw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that in the far future—in the era of isolation—our \npast light cone will reach a limit in the amount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stuff it can encompass. Our observable universe \nwill be around 64 billion light years in radius,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696720,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to the 46.5 billion light years of the \npresent. These are comoving units—so eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll be able to see light from objects that \nare currently 64 billion light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709320,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we really are going to launch our \nfleets and spread through the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713580,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future humans will have different past light \ncones. Explorers and their descendents who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719760,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leave soon and don’t stop, traveling at close \nto light speed until the era of isolation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725490,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will see the Milky Way in the distances in one \ndirection, and their observable universe will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731100,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be shifted 16.5 billion light years in the other \ndirection, encompassing billions of galaxies that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737280,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way stay-at-homes will never see. We can \ndefine a region of space that we can possibly one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744780,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day observe as the combination of all past light \ncones of all possible light speed explorers. Ord  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752100,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calls this the ultimately observable universe, \nand it’s nearly 160 billion light years across."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757740,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although we may one day fill a vast region of \nthe cosmos and see even more of it, we won’t be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763800,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to tell each other what we found. Or even \nreally call ourselves a cosmic civilization.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our far future descendents will be scattered \nacross many millions of causally disconnected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions, beyond each other's event horizons. \nNo communication is possible between them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782040,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so presumably over the billions of years these \noutposts must diverge in culture and appearance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788460,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that isolation begins much earlier. In a 2022 \npaper, S. Jay Olson studied the capacity for an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796620,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding civilization to maintain contact across \nits ever-increasing extent. He calculated the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803280,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of times an expanding civilization can have \ntwo-way communication before the era of isolation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810240,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s use our spacetime diagram again. A colony \nship travels outwards at, say, 20% light speed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816900,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They send intermittent messages back home, which \ntravel at light speed—as does the reply message.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824040,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If some of the explorers just keep \ntraveling then it takes a while for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828300,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reply to catch up to them. The total \nnumber of back and forth messages depends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833760,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on how far each colony group manages \nto travel before the era of isolation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our average speed is 20% light speed, then \na large fraction of our ultimate spread can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have multiple communications with home, although \nmore than half of the volume will have had 2 or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851400,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fewer communications. There’s an extensive \nregion at the rim that has been traveling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856500,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too fast to ever get the return message from \nany of its communications. But if the average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed is closer to light speed, then the \nvast majority of expanding settlers never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869640,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heard anything back from Earth in the billions \nof years of their travel. And they never will."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875220,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Humanity’s future may be very, very large. If \nwe want it to be. Maybe we’ll fill 20 billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883260,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies with life and mind. Maybe we’ll realize \nthere are even better ways to spend eternity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888600,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we do decide to expand, then we should try \nto maximize our chance of success. I would argue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896040,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that flinging colony ships in all directions \nas soon as possible is not necessarily the way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901380,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to go. We have a billion years to fine tune our \nintergalactic plans without missing too much of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907200,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe. And, remember, Whoever we send will \nhave very limited contact with home base, and so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the state of humanity at the moment of launch will \nform the seed of our cosmic legacy. “The sky calls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to us. If we do not destroy ourselves, we will \none day venture to the stars” so said Carl Sagan.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927720,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmic extent of our species is potentially \nenormous. It’ll be a hell of a responsibility,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934380,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we do choose to send our descendents \nbeyond the horizons of our local space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941580,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we wrap up, we wanted to let you know \nit’s Earth Month across PBS! As you can see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947220,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developing the science for light speed travel \nand building spaceships capable of traveling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952200,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the distant universe is going to be \nquite difficult. So it's going to be a good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957420,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea that we take care of our home planet \nuntil the time when we're ready for launch.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962580,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And taking care of and appreciating our blue \nmarble is a big theme of Earth Month across PBS.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One new series you might want to check \nout is called Untold Earth on PBS Terra.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975600,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A collaboration between Atlas Obscura and PBS \nNATURE, Untold Earth explores the mysteries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982020,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind North America’s strangest and most unique \nnatural wonders. We have a link below to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988140,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first episode on redwoods as well as link to the \ncomplete (and growing) Earth Month playlist. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994980,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you travel to other channels, please make sure \nto tell them, politely, that Space Time sent you!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
